1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:10,519
In 1993, three engineers sit at a booth in a roadside diner in San Jose, California. They order coffee. Lots of coffee.

2
00:00:10,739 --> 00:00:18,278
One of them, Jensen Huang, knows that chain well: at fifteen, he had washed dishes at one of its locations.

3
00:00:18,828 --> 00:00:25,366
They want to build a chip so video games can be seen in three dimensions. They start with forty thousand dollars.

4
00:00:25,586 --> 00:00:33,817
Thirty-three years later, that company reached nearly six trillion dollars in value. No company had ever been worth that much.

5
00:00:34,467 --> 00:00:37,544
And this week, the man from that booth stepped down.

6
00:00:38,394 --> 00:00:43,855
This is the story of NVIDIA. And of a question that no one can answer today.

7
00:00:44,705 --> 00:00:47,705
CHAPTER 1 — THE WRONG CHIP

8
00:00:48,055 --> 00:00:57,132
The name came from invidia, the Latin word for envy. And the first chip, in 1995, was called the NV1.

9
00:00:57,352 --> 00:01:04,660
It had a problem. It drew objects using curved surfaces. And the rest of the industry chose triangles.

10
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:12,880
When Microsoft built its standard for Windows games, it built it around triangles. The NV1 was left on the wrong side.

11
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:20,254
NVIDIA had a contract with Sega for the chip in its next console. But it was still using the same wrong technology.

12
00:01:21,104 --> 00:01:24,488
The company had enough cash left for about thirty days.

13
00:01:25,338 --> 00:01:27,877
CHAPTER 2 — THIRTY DAYS

14
00:01:28,227 --> 00:01:34,150
Huang flew to Japan to meet Sega's president, Shoichiro Irimajiri. And he told him the truth.

15
00:01:34,370 --> 00:01:45,447
He told him their chip wouldn't work and that Sega should find another partner. And he asked for one more thing: that the final payment of the contract, five million dollars, remain as an investment in NVIDIA.

16
00:01:45,667 --> 00:01:53,667
According to Huang, he warned him that he would most likely lose that money. Irimajiri thought about it for a couple of days and agreed.

17
00:01:53,887 --> 00:02:05,887
With that money they had about six months. They bet everything on a new chip built with triangles: the RIVA 128. It sold one million units in four months.

18
00:02:06,737 --> 00:02:15,045
Ever since, Huang opened many meetings with the same line: we are thirty days from going out of business. Remember that phrase.

19
00:02:15,595 --> 00:02:23,210
Sega sold its shares when NVIDIA went public. According to Huang, today they would be worth nearly one trillion dollars.

20
00:02:24,060 --> 00:02:26,829
CHAPTER 3 — THOUSANDS OF HANDS

21
00:02:27,179 --> 00:02:39,102
In 1999, NVIDIA went public and launched the GeForce 256. They sold it as the world's first GPU. And what is a GPU?

22
00:02:39,552 --> 00:02:49,937
Let's go to the chalkboard. A regular screen has about two million tiny dots, the pixels. And in a game, each one can change color sixty times per second.

23
00:02:50,157 --> 00:02:58,695
A computer's processor, the CPU, is like a brilliant professor. It solves any problem, but only a few at a time.

24
00:02:58,915 --> 00:03:07,685
A GPU is like a classroom with thousands of students. Each one only knows how to do simple math. But they all do it at the same time.

25
00:03:07,905 --> 00:03:13,443
To paint millions of pixels you don't need a genius. You need thousands of hands.

26
00:03:14,293 --> 00:03:19,139
A CPU thinks fast. A GPU thinks in parallel.

27
00:03:19,989 --> 00:03:23,528
CHAPTER 4 — THE BET NOBODY WANTED

28
00:03:23,878 --> 00:03:33,493
In 2006, Huang made an unusual bet. It was called CUDA, and it allowed graphics cards to be used for any calculation, not just games.

29
00:03:33,713 --> 00:03:41,482
Scientists and programmers could put those thousands of hands to work on whatever they wanted: physics, weather, medicine.

30
00:03:41,702 --> 00:03:47,164
Wall Street didn't like it. It cost a fortune, and nobody could see how it would make money.

31
00:03:47,384 --> 00:03:53,461
For years, the company was worth around ten billion dollars, and barely moved.

32
00:03:54,311 --> 00:03:57,772
It was a bridge toward a city that didn't exist yet.

33
00:03:58,622 --> 00:04:02,161
CHAPTER 5 — TWO CARDS IN A BEDROOM

34
00:04:02,511 --> 00:04:13,203
In 2012, a student in Toronto named Alex Krizhevsky built something in his bedroom at his parents' house. He put two gaming graphics cards into his computer.

35
00:04:13,423 --> 00:04:27,808
With his colleague Ilya Sutskever and his professor Geoffrey Hinton—remember these characters from our first episode on the history of AI?—he trained a neural network to recognize photos. It took about six days.

36
00:04:28,028 --> 00:04:35,643
He entered an image recognition competition and won by a massive margin. The network was called AlexNet.

37
00:04:36,093 --> 00:04:43,324
Why a gaming card? To the chalkboard. A neural network learns by multiplying huge tables of numbers.

38
00:04:43,544 --> 00:04:50,852
They are millions of simple multiplications, all at once. The exact same thing a GPU does to move the triangles in a game.

39
00:04:51,072 --> 00:04:58,687
The classroom of thousands of students could also teach a machine. And thanks to CUDA, anyone could hand it the assignment.

40
00:04:59,537 --> 00:05:04,460
Without looking for it, the video game chip was the artificial intelligence chip.

41
00:05:05,310 --> 00:05:08,233
CHAPTER 6 — THE GOLD RUSH

42
00:05:08,583 --> 00:05:21,352
In 2016, Huang personally delivered a supercomputer to a small lab in San Francisco. It was NVIDIA's first for artificial intelligence. The lab was called OpenAI.

43
00:05:21,572 --> 00:05:30,111
Six years later, that lab launched ChatGPT. And every company in the world wanted the same thing: NVIDIA chips.

44
00:05:30,331 --> 00:05:34,485
It was a gold rush. And NVIDIA was selling the shovels.

45
00:05:34,705 --> 00:05:43,705
In 2023 it passed one trillion dollars in value. And in 2025 it became the first company in history to pass five trillion.

46
00:05:44,355 --> 00:06:00,585
There were scares. In January 2025, a cheaper Chinese model, DeepSeek, spooked investors. In a single day, NVIDIA lost nearly six hundred billion dollars in value. No company had ever lost that much in one day.

47
00:06:00,805 --> 00:06:06,498
And NVIDIA kept climbing. In October 2026, it nearly touched six trillion.

48
00:06:07,348 --> 00:06:12,502
Thirty-three years after that diner booth, it was the most valuable company on the planet.

49
00:06:13,352 --> 00:06:16,582
CHAPTER 7 — WHAT A COMPANY IS WORTH

50
00:06:16,932 --> 00:06:22,855
Before the ending, one last chalkboard. What does it mean for a company to be worth six trillion dollars?

51
00:06:23,075 --> 00:06:30,922
First, the word. In Spanish, a billón is a million millions. In English, it's called a trillion.

52
00:06:31,142 --> 00:06:40,065
A publicly traded company is divided into tiny pieces, called shares. Its value is the price of one share multiplied by the total number of shares.

53
00:06:40,285 --> 00:06:47,438
That price isn't cash locked inside a vault. It's what people believe the company will earn in the future.

54
00:06:47,658 --> 00:06:55,658
That's why it can lose hundreds of billions without a single machine breaking. All it takes is a change in what people believe.

55
00:06:56,508 --> 00:07:01,278
A company isn't worth what it has. It's worth what people believe it will have.

56
00:07:02,128 --> 00:07:04,512
CHAPTER 8 — THE WEEK

57
00:07:04,862 --> 00:07:14,939
And people began to doubt. Since October, the stock had been falling. Concerns grew over the debt many companies are taking on to buy artificial intelligence chips.

58
00:07:15,589 --> 00:07:28,820
Then, on a weekend in November, The New York Times published a story about Huang. According to the paper, he had been in a relationship with a woman whom U.S. intelligence considered a Chinese agent.

59
00:07:29,040 --> 00:07:42,502
Huang acknowledged in a letter to employees that he had been unfaithful to his wife. The board opened an investigation into whether he had been a target of espionage, and said it had no reason to believe any secrets were leaked.

60
00:07:42,722 --> 00:07:51,568
Afterward, The New York Times and Reuters reported other relationships between Huang and employees. One of them was an executive who reported to him.

61
00:07:52,418 --> 00:08:01,725
On Thursday, November 12, NVIDIA announced his resignation. After thirty-three years, the founder stepped down as CEO and left the board.

62
00:08:01,945 --> 00:08:08,330
Chief financial officer Colette Kress took over as interim CEO while the company searches for a replacement.

63
00:08:08,550 --> 00:08:17,704
In addition, three U.S. House committees summoned him to testify behind closed doors. The hearing is set for December 1.

64
00:08:18,604 --> 00:08:22,296
In a single week, NVIDIA was left without its founder.

65
00:08:23,146 --> 00:08:25,377
CHAPTER 9 — THE END?

66
00:08:25,727 --> 00:08:36,650
Since October, NVIDIA has lost nearly 1.7 trillion dollars in value. Six straight weeks of decline: its worst streak since 2012.

67
00:08:36,870 --> 00:08:45,101
Meanwhile, the chips keep selling. For this quarter, the company expected to bring in around 108 billion dollars in revenue.

68
00:08:45,851 --> 00:08:57,312
For thirty-three years, Huang repeated that they were thirty days from going out of business. Since 1996, it was never true again. It was his way of never relaxing.

69
00:08:57,532 --> 00:09:04,532
Today NVIDIA is not thirty days from going out of business. But for the first time since that diner booth, it doesn't have its founder.

70
00:09:05,432 --> 00:09:10,740
Is this the end of the NVIDIA story? Or is it just another one of those thirty days?

71
00:09:11,690 --> 00:09:18,075
What do you think? What other story would you like to see in the next episode? Leave it down in the comments.
